\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Chapter 7: Estimating Systems of Equations by OLS and GLS
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Wooldridge Slides
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Introduction to Panel Data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Preliminaries
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We assume random sampling of units from a well-define population.
 We only concentrate on the panel data case, so we sample different time
 periods on the same response and explanatory variables.
 Also, we assume a small number of time periods; to apply standard limit
 theorems, we can use results for independent, identically distributed observati
ons.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We suppose that, for each unit in the population, we have a (usually short)
 time series, 
\begin_inset Formula $\{(x_{t},y_{t}):t=1,\ldots,T\}$
\end_inset

.
 A linear panel data model is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{t}=x_{t}^{\prime}\beta+u_{t},\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

In this setup, there is a single response variable, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

, that we observe in several time periods.
 Having the same 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is not restrictive because 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}$
\end_inset

 can be very general.
 It can (and usually should) include time-period dummies to allow a different
 intercept in each period.
 It can include interactions between time period dummies and other variables
 to allow effects to change over time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For panel data, we can write equation (1) in system form (for a random draw
 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

) as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=X_{i}\beta+u_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
y_{i1} & \cdots & y_{iT}\end{array}\right]_{T\times1}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
u_{i1} & \cdots & u_{iT}\end{array}\right]_{T\times1}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X_{i}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
x_{i1}^{\prime}\\
\vdots\\
x_{it}^{\prime}
\end{array}\right]_{T\times K}
\]

\end_inset

where each 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{it}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

.
 So, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X_{i}^{\prime}X_{i}=\left[\begin{array}{ccc}
x_{i1} & \cdots & x_{iT}\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
x_{i1}^{\prime}\\
\vdots\\
x_{it}^{\prime}
\end{array}\right]={\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Exogeneity Assumptions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An atractive assumption in the general equation 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=x_{t}^{\prime}\beta+u_{t}$
\end_inset

 is 
\series bold
contemporaneous exogeneity
\series default
:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{t}|x_{t})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which implies
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(y_{t}|x_{t})=x_{t}^{\prime}\beta,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This assumption rules out omitted variables, measurement error, and so on,
 but it does not restrict correlation between 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{s}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $s\neq t$
\end_inset

.
 Stated as a zero conditional mean, it also implies correct functional form
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{t}|x_{t})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An assumption that is stronger than (3), but can be justified when we assume
 the dynamics are completely specified (with respect to the elements of
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}$
\end_inset

) is 
\series bold
sequential exogeneity
\series default
:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{t}|x_{t},x_{t-1},\ldots,x_{1})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As we will see, (5) can be applied to distributed lag models and models
 with lagged dependent variables.
 (5) implies
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(y_{t}|x_{t},x_{t-1},\ldots,x_{1})=E(y_{t}|x_{t})=x_{t}^{\prime}\beta
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which makes it clear that enough lags of whatever have been included in
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}$
\end_inset

 so that no further lags are needed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An even stronger assumption, both technically and practically, is 
\series bold
strict exogeneity
\series default
:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{t}|x_{1},\ldots,x_{t-1},x_{t},x_{t+1},\ldots,x_{T})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{s}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 are uncorrelated for all 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 (7) is the same as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(y_{t}|x_{1},\ldots,x_{t-1},x_{t},x_{t+1},\ldots,x_{T})=E(y_{t}|x_{t})=x_{t}^{\prime}\beta
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the first equality is the critical one.
 It says that, given whatever is in 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}$
\end_inset

, the covariates from other time periods do not help explain 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 The main concern with (8) is that it rules out feedback from shocks to
 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 to future outcomes on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 In particular, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t+1}$
\end_inset

 cannot be correlated.
 So, any model with a lagged dependent variable 
\emph on
must
\emph default
 violate strict exogeneity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
System OLS (SOLS)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Consistency
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Assumptions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Assumption SOLS.1
\series default
:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(X_{i}^{\prime}u_{i})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This is the weakest possible assumption without moving into instrumental
 variables territory.
 Clearly, (9) is implied by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{i}|X_{i})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

almost the strongest assumption we could make.
 For panel data,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
X_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}={\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}u_{it}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and so SOLS.1 is the same as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}E(x_{it}u_{it})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

It is unlikely we would assume (12) without just assuming
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(x_{it}u_{it})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\]

\end_inset

So, SOLS.1 for panel data is contemporaneous exogeneity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Assumption SOLS.2
\series default
:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{rank}E(X_{i}^{\prime}X_{i})=K
\end{equation}

\end_inset

For panel data, SOLS.2 holds if and only if
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{rank}\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}E(x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime})\right]=K
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Population Orthogonality Conditions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E\left[X_{i}^{\prime}\left(y_{i}-X_{i}\beta\right)\right] & = & 0\ \mbox{by SOLS.1}\\
E(X_{i}^{\prime}X_{i})\beta & = & E(X_{i}^{\prime}y_{i})\\
\beta & = & \left[E(X_{i}^{\prime}X_{i})\right]^{-1}E(X_{i}^{\prime}y_{i})\ \mbox{by SOLS.2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
SOLS Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta}_{SOLS}=\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}X_{i}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}y_{i}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which can be writeen as 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{SOLS}=(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}Y$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $NT\times K$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $NT\times1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*
Under SOLS.1 and SOLS.2, OLS on a random sample is consistent:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{p\lim}_{N\rightarrow\infty}\left(\hat{\beta}_{SOLS}\right)=\beta
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
System OLS is not unbiased under SOLS.1 and SOLS.2.
 It is if we use
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{i}|X_{i})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

but this is very strong.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For panel data, we assume fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 growing.
 Reflects most micro panel data sets: many units, few time periods per unit.
 The fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 scenario is very convenient: it means the limit theorems are for random
 sampling.
 Further, the results allow for 
\emph on
any
\emph default
 kind of time series dependence in the explanatory variables or errors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Asymptotic Normality and Inference
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Asymptotic Normality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}(\hat{\beta}-\beta)=\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}X_{i}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}\right)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By the CLT for i.i.d.
 random vectors
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
\left(N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}\right)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}N(0,B)\\
B=\mbox{Var}(X_{i}^{\prime}u_{i})=E(X_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}X_{i})
\end{gather}

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
A=E(X_{i}^{\prime}X_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\sqrt{N}(\hat{\beta}-\beta) & = & A^{-1}\left(N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}\right)+\left[\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}X_{i}\right)^{-1}-A^{-1}\right]\left(N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}\right)\\
 & = & A^{-1}\left(N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}u_{i}\right)+o_{p}(1)\cdot O_{p}(1)\nonumber 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

So,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sqrt{N}(\hat{\beta}-\beta)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}N(0,A^{-1}BA^{-1})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The SOLS residuals are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{u}_{i}=y_{i}-X_{i}\hat{\beta}\ (\mbox{each}\ T\times1)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Variance Estimators
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A fully robust estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{B}=(N-K)^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\hat{u}_{i}\hat{u}_{i}^{\prime}X_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The resulting sandwich estimator is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta})}=\frac{N}{N-K}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}X_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\hat{u}_{i}\hat{u}_{i}^{\prime}X_{i}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}X_{i}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For panel data, (24) allows the variances, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(u_{t})$
\end_inset

, and covariances, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Cov}(u_{t},u_{s})$
\end_inset

, to be unrestricted.
 Thus, it is robust to arbitrary heteroskedasticity as a function of time
 and to any serial correlation pattern in the errors, 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ u_{it}:t=1,\ldots,T\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 In addition, it is robust to the conditional variances and covariances
 being any function of 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ x_{it}:t=1,\ldots,T\right\} $
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Testing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Obtain asymptotic standard errors form (24) to construct large-sample 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 statistics (which converge to standard normal under null as 
\begin_inset Formula $N\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

) and confidence intervals.
 Consider hypotheses
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
H_{0}:R\beta=r
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $Q\times K$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $Q\times1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $Q\leq K$
\end_inset

.
 Most convenient is the 
\series bold
Wald statisti
\series default
c
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
W=\left(R\hat{\beta}-r\right)^{\prime}\left[R\hat{V}R^{\prime}\right]^{-1}\left(R\hat{\beta}-r\right)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{a}\\
\sim
\end{array}\chi^{2}(Q)
\]

\end_inset

under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{V}$
\end_inset

 is given in (24).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Pooled OLS (POLS)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Consistency Assumptions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Assumption POLS.1
\series default
 (Contemporaneous Exogeneity):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(x_{it}u_{it})=0,\ t=1,\ldots,T
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Remember, POLS.1 allows for lagged dependent variables as well as other non-stric
tly exogenous regressors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Assumption POLS.2
\series default
 (Rank Condition):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{rank}\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}E\left(x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)\right]=K
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under POLS.1 and POLS.2, the 
\series bold
pooled OLS estimator
\series default
 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{POLS}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}y_{it}\right)
\]

\end_inset

and the asymptotic variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}\left(\hat{\beta}_{POLS}-\beta\right)$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}E\left(x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}{\displaystyle \sum_{s=1}^{T}}E\left(u_{it}u_{is}x_{it}x_{is}^{\prime}\right)\right]\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}E\left(x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This expression simplifies if we appropriately restrict the conditional
 variances and covariances.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Homoskedasticity and No Serial Correlation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Assumption POLS.3
\series default
 (Homoskedasticity and No Serial Correlation):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
(a)\ E\left(u_{it}^{2}x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right) & = & E(u_{it}^{2})E(x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime})\nonumber \\
 & = & \sigma^{2}E(x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}),\ \mbox{where}\ \sigma^{2}=E(u_{it}^{2})\ \forall\ t\\
(b)\ E(u_{it}u_{is}x_{it}x_{is}^{\prime}) & = & 0\ \forall\ t\neq s
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Under POLS.3, (28) becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sigma^{2}\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}E\left(x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

POLS.3 implies that the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

usual
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 asymptotic variance matrix estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{POLS}$
\end_inset

 is valid:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{POLS})} & = & \hat{\sigma}^{2}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}=\hat{\sigma}^{2}(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}\\
\hat{\sigma}^{2} & = & (NT-K)^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\hat{u}_{it}^{2}=\nicefrac{SSR}{NT-K}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

We can use the usual 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 statistics as approximately valid for large 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Without POLS.3, generally need fully robust variance matrix.
 That is, robust to arbitrary heteroskedasticity and serial correlation.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{POLS})}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}{\displaystyle \sum_{s=1}^{T}}\hat{u}_{it}\hat{u}_{is}x_{it}x_{is}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This estimator in Stata is computed using a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

cluster
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 option, where each unit 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is a cluster of 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 time series observations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If we maintain the no serial correlation part of POLS.3, then a heteroskedasticit
y-robust form is valid:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}\hat{u}_{it}^{2}x_{it}x_{is}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{T}}x_{it}x_{it}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

In Stata, this estimator is obtained with a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

robust
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 option, but its robustness is limited to heteroskedasticity, not serial
 correlation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
System Generalized Least Squares (SGLS)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Asymptotic Properties of SGLS
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
SGLS Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

By 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

generalized least squares,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 we mean exploiting different 
\emph on
unconditional 
\emph default
variances across time and nonzero 
\emph on
unconditional
\emph default
 covariances across equations.
 We do not exploit situations where the variance-covariance matrix is a
 function of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times T$
\end_inset

 unconditional variance-covariance matrix plays a key role.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Omega\equiv E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})=\left[\begin{array}{cccc}
\sigma_{1}^{2} & \sigma_{12} & \cdots & \sigma_{1T}\\
\sigma_{12} & \sigma_{2}^{2} & \cdots & \sigma_{2T}\\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
\sigma_{1T} & \sigma_{2T} & \cdots & \sigma_{T}^{2}
\end{array}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The following language is as if 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, which is maintained in virtually all applications and is no assumption
 at all when the various equations contain an intercept.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Assume, for now, that we know 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

.
 Transform the equation to remove the correlations in errors and make variances
 constant
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Omega^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}y_{i}=\Omega^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}X_{i}\beta+\Omega^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}u_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is assumed to be nonsingular and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}$
\end_inset

 is a symmetric matrix such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}\Omega^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}=\Omega^{-1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}\Omega\Omega^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}=I_{T}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}^{*}=\Omega^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}X_{i}$
\end_inset

, and similarly for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}^{*}u_{i}^{*\prime})=\Omega^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})\Omega^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}=I_{T}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Apply system OLS to 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{*}=X_{i}^{*}\beta+u_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 The GLS estimator is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\beta_{GLS}^{*} & = & \left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{*\prime}X_{i}^{*}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{*\prime}y_{i}^{*}\right)\\
 & = & \left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X_{i}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}y_{i}\right)\\
 & = & \beta+\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X_{i}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times K$
\end_inset

 matrix average converges in probability to 
\begin_inset Formula $E(X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X_{i})$
\end_inset

; assume this is nonsingular.
 Then, consistency of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta^{*}$
\end_inset

 holds if
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

In general, (36) is not implied by SOLS.1.
 GLS transforms the orthogonality conditions; it may not be consistent when
 SOLS is.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Consistency Assumptions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Assumption SGLS.1
\series default
 (Strict Exogeneity):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(X_{i}\otimes u_{i})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Every element of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with every element of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

, so (36) holds because any linear combination of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 SGLS.1 implies SOLS.1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Assumption SGLS.2
\series default
 (Rank Condition): 
\begin_inset Formula $E(X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X_{i})$
\end_inset

 is nonsingular
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*
Under SGLS.1 and SGLS.2, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta^{*}$
\end_inset

 is consistent for 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $N\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Asymptotic Normality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Asymptotic normality is straightforward:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\sqrt{N}(\beta^{*}-\beta) & = & \left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X_{i}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i}\right)\\
 & = & A^{-1}\left(N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i}\right)\\
 &  & +\left[\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X_{i}\right)^{-1}-A^{-1}\right]\left(N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i}\right)\\
 & = & A^{-1}\left(N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i}\right)+o_{p}(1)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Now,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
A=E(X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

By the CLT,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
\sqrt{N}(\beta^{*}-\beta)\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{d}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}N(0,A^{-1}BA^{-1})\\
B=\mbox{Var}(X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i})=E(X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X_{i})
\end{gather}

\end_inset

At this point, we cannot simplify 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 further.
 We are not assuming 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 is nonrandom, and we dow not have enough assumptions about the distribution
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 to reduce 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 Under SGLS.1, SOLS.2, and SGLS.2, GLS need 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 be more efficient than SOLS.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Feasible GLS (FGLS)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
The Estimator and Asymptotic Properties
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now we study the estimator from the previous subsection but where an estimator
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is used in place of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

.
 Generally, let 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Omega}$
\end_inset

 be a 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times T$
\end_inset

 matrix such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{p\lim}_{N\rightarrow\infty}\hat{\Omega}=\Omega
\end{equation}

\end_inset

For panel data, we almost always use
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\Omega}=N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\check{u}_{i}\check{u}_{i}^{\prime}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{u}_{i}=y_{i}-X_{i}\check{\beta}$
\end_inset

 are the 
\begin_inset Formula $T\times1$
\end_inset

 POLS residuals (
\begin_inset Formula $\check{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is the POLS estimator).Write
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\check{u}_{i} & = & y_{i}-X_{i}\beta-X_{i}(\check{\beta}-\beta)\nonumber \\
 & = & u_{i}-X_{i}(\check{\beta}-\beta)\nonumber \\
\check{u}_{i}\check{u}_{i}^{\prime} & = & u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}-u_{i}(\check{\beta}-\beta)^{\prime}X_{i}^{\prime}-X_{i}(\check{\beta}-\beta)u_{i}^{\prime}+X_{i}(\check{\beta}-\beta)(\check{\beta}-\beta)^{\prime}X_{i}^{\prime}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

We can show that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\Omega}=N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}+o_{p}(1)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The 
\series bold
FGLS estimator
\series default
 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\hat{\beta}_{FGLS} & = & \left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}X_{i}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}y_{i}\right)\\
 & = & \beta+\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}X_{i}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}u_{i}\right)\nonumber 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Write
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}X_{i}-N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X_{i}=N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\left(\hat{\Omega}^{-1}-\Omega^{-1}\right)X_{i}
\]

\end_inset

From matrix algebra, for conformable matrices 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{vec}(ABC)=(C^{\prime}\otimes A)\mbox{vec}(B)
\]

\end_inset

Under SGLS.1, SGLS.2, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Omega}=N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}+o_{p}(1)$
\end_inset

 ,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mbox{vec}\left[N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\left(\hat{\Omega}^{-1}-\Omega^{-1}\right)X_{i}\right] & = & \left[N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\left(X_{i}^{\prime}\otimes X_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]\mbox{vec}\left(\hat{\Omega}^{-1}-\Omega^{-1}\right)\\
 & = & O_{p}(1)\cdot o_{p}(1)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\left(X_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}u_{i}-X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i}\right) & = & N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\left(\hat{\Omega}^{-1}-\Omega^{-1}\right)u_{i}\\
 & = & \left[N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\left(u_{i}\otimes X_{i}\right)^{\prime}\right]\mbox{vec}\left(\hat{\Omega}^{-1}-\Omega^{-1}\right)\\
 & = & O_{p}(1)\cdot o_{p}(1)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Combining the above two results gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\sqrt{N}\left(\hat{\beta}-\beta\right) & = & \left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X_{i}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i}\right)+o_{p}(1)\\
 & = & \sqrt{N}(\beta^{*}-\beta)+o_{p}(1)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

By the asymptotic equivalence lemma, the asymptotic distribution of 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}\left(\hat{\beta}-\beta\right)$
\end_inset

 is the same as that of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}(\beta^{*}-\beta)$
\end_inset

.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}(\hat{\beta}-\beta^{*})=o_{p}(1)$
\end_inset

 we say that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta^{*}$
\end_inset

 are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}$
\end_inset

- equivalent.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Thus, we can treat FGLS as if it is GLS.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A fully robust sandwish variance matrix estimator can be used under SGLS.1
 and SGLS.2.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{u}_{i}\equiv y_{i}-X_{i}\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 be the FGLS residuals.
 Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta})}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}X_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}\hat{u}_{i}\hat{u}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}X_{i}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}X_{i}\right)^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

This estimator is robust to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

system heteroskedasticity.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Loosely, the variance-covariance matrix of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 doe not depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Homoskedasticity and Efficiency
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Assumption SGLS.3
\series default
 (System Homoskedasticity):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E\left(X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X_{i}\right)=E(X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Effectively, all squares and cross products of elements in 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 are uncorrelated with the squares and cross products of elements in 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 This assumption says that 
\begin_inset Formula $B=A$
\end_inset

, which means we can use
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta})}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}X_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\Omega}^{-1}X_{i}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which is the nonrobust (
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

usual
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

) FGLS variance matrix estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Sufficient for SGLS.3
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}|X_{i})=E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Use the law of iterated expectations
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E\left(X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X_{i}\right) & = & E\left[E\left(X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X_{i}\right)|X_{i}\right]\\
 & = & E\left[\left(X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}E\left(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}|X_{i}\right)\Omega^{-1}X_{i}\right)\right]\\
 & = & E\left(X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}\Omega\Omega^{-1}X_{i}\right)\\
 & = & E(X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X_{i})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Most straightforward and traditional
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
E(u_{i}|X_{i})=0\\
\mbox{Var}(u_{i}|X_{i})=\mbox{Var}(u_{i})=\Omega
\end{gather}

\end_inset

Given the zero conditional mean assumption, the key conditions are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\mbox{Var}(u_{it}|X_{i})=\mbox{Var}(u_{it}),\ t=1,\ldots,T\\
\mbox{Cov}(u_{it},u_{is}|X_{i})=\mbox{Cov}(u_{it},u_{is}),\ \forall\ t\neq s
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

By the random sampling assumption, the unconditional variance-covarce matrices
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 must be identical across 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, and equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

.
 The question is whether conditional variance and covariances conditional
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 are constant.
 Particularly in panel data applications without strict exogenetiy it will
 not make sense to condition on all of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
When is FGLS more efficient than SOLS?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose we start with SGLS.1 (which implies SOLS.1), SOLS.2 and SGLS.2, and
 this form of the system homoskedasticity assumption:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime}|X_{i})=E(u_{i}u_{i}^{\prime})
\]

\end_inset

Then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{FGLS}) & = & N^{-1}\cdot\left[E(X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X_{i})\right]^{-1}\\
\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta}_{SOLS}) & = & N^{-1}\cdot\left[E(X_{i}^{\prime}X_{i})\right]^{-1}E(X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega X_{i})\left[E(X_{i}^{\prime}X_{i})\right]^{-1}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim*

\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[E(X_{i}^{\prime}X_{i})\right]^{-1}E(X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega X_{i})\left[E(X_{i}^{\prime}X_{i})\right]^{-1}-\left[E(X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X_{i})\right]^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

is positive semi-definite.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Write the difference as 
\begin_inset Formula $C-D$
\end_inset

.
 It sufficies to show 
\begin_inset Formula $D^{-1}-C^{-1}$
\end_inset

 is p.s.d., that is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X_{i})-E(X_{i}^{\prime}X_{i})\left[E(X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega X_{i})\right]^{-1}E(X_{i}^{\prime}X_{i})
\]

\end_inset

is p.s.d.
 Use the following trick.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{i}\equiv\Omega^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}X_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{i}\equiv\Omega^{\nicefrac{1}{2}}X_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(Z_{i}^{\prime}Z_{i})=E(X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega^{-1}X_{i})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(W_{i}^{\prime}W_{i})=E(X_{i}^{\prime}\Omega X_{i})$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(Z_{i}^{\prime}W_{i})=E(X_{i}^{\prime}X_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, the difference in the equation above is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(Z_{i}^{\prime}Z_{i})-E(Z_{i}^{\prime}W_{i})\left[E(W_{i}^{\prime}W_{i})\right]^{-1}E(Z_{i}^{\prime}W_{i})
\]

\end_inset

which looks like a matrix sum of squared residuals in the population.
 In fact, if we define the matrix residuals 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{i}=Z_{i}-W_{i}\Pi$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi\equiv\left[E(W_{i}^{\prime}W_{i})\right]^{-1}E(Z_{i}^{\prime}W_{i})$
\end_inset

, then we have 
\begin_inset Formula $E(R_{i}^{\prime}R_{i})$
\end_inset

, which is necessarily p.s.d.
\end_layout

